@jimhester would you consider this PR to include internal R6 objects?

I expect that it will have a pretty big impact on anyone using `covr` together with R6, but I feel it's the "right" thing to do and is consistent with the way `covr` currently handles internal functions like `.function_a()`.

Uploading reports to [Codecov.io](https://codecov.io/)

Supported `json` format:

``` json
{
  "uploader": "R",
  "files": [
    {
      "name": "string",
      "coverage": [null, 0, null, 1]
    }
  ]
}
```

> Branch coverage is supported via `"1/2"` for partial coverage or `"2/2"` for full branch coverage. This is optional if R can produce this data.

My first R lang experience. Help me fix any issues presented. Special thanks to @daroczig and @RomanTsegelskyi

`subprocess()` would generate R scripts that are invalid on Windows due to backslashes in temporary pathnames, e.g.

``` r
library(methods)
load('C:\Users\hb\AppData\Local\Temp\RtmpqUOxz3\file160c18a84d19')
.exprs <- readRDS('C:\Users\hb\AppData\Local\Temp\RtmpqUOxz3\file160ceb71f94')
fun <- function() {
  .output <- capture.output(
    for(.expr in .exprs) {
      eval(.expr)
    }
  )
  saveRDS(file = 'C:\Users\hb\AppData\Local\Temp\RtmpqUOxz3\file160c348d338a', .output)
  .new_objs <- ls(environment())
  if (length(.new_objs) > 0) {
    save(list = .new_objs, file = 'C:\Users\hb\AppData\Local\Temp\RtmpqUOxz3\file160c176e507e')
  }
  .new_objs
}
.env <- readRDS('C:\Users\hb\AppData\Local\Temp\RtmpqUOxz3\file160c26313981')
environment(fun) <- .env
fun()
```

Here I take the simple approach to fix the problem after the `command` string has been created. The alternative is to escape/translate the backslashes of each path before passing them to `sprintf()`.  I also added the test for Windows to make the code more clear on what's going on.

- improved coverage for blocks with simple expressions, such as:
  function(x) { x }
  function() {
  x <- 1
  x
  }

so now expressions such as your a() function:
a <- function(x) {
if (x <= 1) {
1
} else {
2
}
}
can be covered exactly (i.e the "1" and "2" expressions).

I also fixed the coverage statistics that assumed the unicity of first_line attributes. I added a test with a case with nested expressions which have same first_line.

I added some tests, fixed the legacy ones, and provided a Makefile that automates testing with the docker rocker/r-base.

In conclusion: with these modifications, your package give the same results that my patched R, results that were checked during a year, so that's really encouraging.

If you find this pull request interesting, we could discuss some further optimizations.

The "Coverage badge without a coverage service" section was long and sat above Usage. This:

- ships the complete GitHub Actions workflow as `inst/workflows/test-coverage-badge.yaml` (available with `system.file("workflows", "test-coverage-badge.yaml", package = "covr")`), based on the r-lib/actions test-coverage example plus the badge-publishing step;
- replaces the README section with a short FAQ entry, "Can I get a coverage badge without a coverage service?", linking to the file;
- points `?to_badge` at the file instead of the README.
